\par \pard\plain \s42\rtlch\af9\afs21\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe2052\hich\af4\loch\sl-270\slmult0\ql\widctlpar\sb0\sa0\ltrpar\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\f4\fs21\cf0\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af9\langfe2052\qj\ri0\rin0{\loch
only to intense mechanical stimuli, but also to heat and to noxious chemicals. These receptors respond to minute punctures of the epithelium, with a response magnitude that depends on the degree of tissue deformation. They also respond to temperatures in the range of 40\u8211\'9660\u176\'b0C, and change their response rates as a linear function of warming (in contrast with the saturating responses displayed by non-noxious thermoreceptors at high temperatures).}
\par \pard\plain \s42\rtlch\af9\afs21\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe2052\hich\af4\loch\sl-270\slmult0\ql\widctlpar\sb0\sa0\ltrpar\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\f4\fs21\cf0\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af9\langfe2052\qj\fi250\ri0\rin0{\loch
Pain signals can be separated into individual components, corresponding to different types of nerve fibers used for transmitting these signals. The rapidly transmitted signal, which often has high spatial resolution, is called }{\rtlch\ai \ltrch\loch\i\loch
first pain}{\loch
 or }{\rtlch\ai \ltrch\loch\i\loch
cutaneous pricking pain}{\loch
. It is well localized and easily tolerated. The much slower, highly affective component is called }{\rtlch\ai \ltrch\loch\i\loch
second pain}{\loch
 or }{\rtlch\ai \ltrch\loch\i\loch
burning pain}{\loch
; it is poorly localized and poorly tolerated. The third or }{\rtlch\ai \ltrch\loch\i\loch
deep pain}{\loch
, arising from viscera, musculature and joints, is also poorly localized, can be chronic and is often associated with referred pain.}
\par \pard\plain \s1\rtlch\af8\afs28 \ltrch\hich\af6\loch\sl-300\slmult0\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar1\sb280\sa160\keepn\ltrpar\cf1\f6\fs28\dbch\af8\ri0\rin0{\loch
