\par \pard\plain \s42\rtlch\af9\afs21\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe2052\hich\af4\loch\sl-270\slmult0\ql\widctlpar\sb0\sa0\ltrpar\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\f4\fs21\cf0\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af9\langfe2052\qj\li0\lin0{\loch
few myofilaments and is non-contractile, but it does have one or more sensory endings applied to it. When the muscle is stretched, the central part of the intrafusal fiber is stretched and each sensory ending fires impulses.}
\par \pard\plain \s42\rtlch\af9\afs21\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe2052\hich\af4\loch\sl-270\slmult0\ql\widctlpar\sb0\sa0\ltrpar\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\f4\fs21\cf0\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af9\langfe2052\qj\fi250\li0\lin0{\loch
Muscle spindles also receive a motor innervation. The large motor neurons that supply extrafusal muscle fibers are called }{\rtlch\ai \ltrch\loch\i\loch
alpha motor neurons}{\loch
, while the smaller ones supplying the contractile portions of intrafusal fibers are called }{\rtlch\ai \ltrch\loch\i\loch
gamma neurons}{\loch
. Gamma motor neurons can regulate the sensitivity of the muscle spindle so that this sensitivity can be maintained at any given muscle length.}
\par \pard\plain \s1\rtlch\af8\afs28 \ltrch\hich\af6\loch\sl-300\slmult0\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar1\sb280\sa160\keepn\ltrpar\cf1\f6\fs28\dbch\af8\li0\lin0{\loch
Joint receptors}
\par \pard\plain \s42\rtlch\af9\afs21\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe2052\hich\af4\loch\sl-270\slmult0\ql\widctlpar\sb0\sa0\ltrpar\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\f4\fs21\cf0\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af9\langfe2052\qj\li0\lin0{\loch
The joint receptors are low-threshold mechanoreceptors and have been divided into four groups. They signal different characteristics of joint function (position, movements, direction and speed of movements). The free receptors or type 4 joint receptors are nociceptors.}
